-## Monitoring
+## Target limiter
+
+{{% available_from "v1.151.0" %}}
+
+By default, `vmagent` doesn't limit the number of active scrape targets a single scrape job (`job_name`)
+can have at once. This means that if a service discovery source (Kubernetes API, Consul catalog, cloud API, etc.)
+suddenly returns an abnormally large number of targets for a job because of a misconfiguration on the discovery side
+(for example, an accidentally removed tag filter or an overly broad catalog query), `vmagent` will try to scrape
+all of them at once, which can spike CPU and memory usage and degrade scraping for every other job running
+on the same `vmagent` instance.
+
+The maximum number of active targets a single job can have can be enforced in the following places:
+
+* Via `-promscrape.targetsLimitPerJob` command-line flag. This default limit is applied individually
+  to every job defined in the file pointed to by `-promscrape.config`.
+* Via `target_limit` config option at [scrape_config](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/#scrape_configs) section.
+  The `target_limit` allows overriding the `-promscrape.targetsLimitPerJob` on a per-`scrape_config` basis.
+  If `target_limit` is set to `0` or to a negative value, then it isn't applied to the given `scrape_config`,
+  even if the `-promscrape.targetsLimitPerJob` command-line flag is set.
+
+The limit is applied uniformly to the targets produced for the job, no matter which [service discovery
+mechanism(s)](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/sd_configs/) supplied them - a job combining
+multiple discovery mechanisms (for example, `kubernetes_sd_configs` and `static_configs`) is still capped
+at a single `target_limit` in total.
+
+If the number of discovered targets for a job exceeds its `target_limit`, `vmagent` keeps scraping only
+up to `target_limit` of them, while the rest are dropped instead of being scraped. This is a safety net:
+`vmagent` continues operating normally for this and all the other jobs instead of crashing, rejecting the
+whole job, or silently scraping every discovered target.
+
+The targets excluded because of the limit are shown with the `target_limit` reason at the
+`http://vmagent:8429/service-discovery` page and at the `droppedTargets` list of the
+`http://vmagent:8429/api/v1/targets` page, in the same way as targets dropped by
+[relabeling](https://docs.victoriametrics.com/victoriametrics/relabeling/) or by
+[cluster sharding](#scraping-big-number-of-targets) are shown. The `/service-discovery` page also shows,
+next to each job name, the number of active targets out of the total number of discovered targets
+(`<active>/<total> active`), which makes it easy to spot a job that is currently over its `target_limit`.
+
+Which specific targets out of the discovered set are scraped when the limit is exceeded is decided on a
+best-effort basis and may change between service discovery refreshes; already-running targets aren't
+proactively evicted just because `target_limit` is later lowered below the current active count.
 
 `vmagent` exports various metrics in Prometheus exposition format at `http://vmagent-host:8429/metrics` page.
 We recommend setting up regular scraping of this page either through `vmagent` itself or by a Prometheus-compatible scraper,
